5. PRODUCT DEFINITION
Name: MARS//PATH. Tagline: Plan the path. Follow the evidence. Explore Mars.
Positioning: a data-fusion and decision-support layer between planetary science datasets and human Marswalk planning. Mars Trek is the global/regional exploration portal; MARS//PATH is the hyperlocal layer it does not provide:
PLANETARY DATA → LOCALIZED EVIDENCE → SCIENCE TARGET → TERRAIN ANALYSIS → ROUTE TRADEOFF → MISSION DECISION
User questions the UI must always answer: Where am I? What am I looking at? Why does it matter? What can I do next?
Demo region: Gale Crater / Mount Sharp (heavy Curiosity coverage, rich geology, strong orbital data) unless the repo justifies a better one. Default objective: investigate geological evidence of ancient water activity. Alternate objective (include): boxwork formations on lower Mount Sharp, matching the challenge example. Objectives are data-driven and switchable; no objective is hardcoded into logic.
6. DATA ARCHITECTURE
NASA / USGS → ADAPTERS → NORMALIZATION → MARS//PATH MODEL → CACHE → API → FRONTEND
• The frontend never calls NASA/USGS directly (one allowed exception: map tile requests to verified public tile servers, centralized in a single config module).
• Each adapter: typed input/output, schema validation (Zod or equivalent), timeouts, retry with backoff, rate-limit respect, structured errors, attribution passthrough.
• Every normalized record carries a Provenance object:
Provenance {
  sourceId; sourceName; mission; instrument; productId?;
  sourceUrl; retrievedAt; processing: string[];   // e.g. "bilinear resample", "slope via Horn 3x3"
  license; attribution; status: 'LIVE'|'CACHED'|'DERIVED'|'SIMULATION';
  confidence?: 'HIGH'|'MEDIUM'|'LOW'; limitations: string[];
}
• Dataset model: id, name, type (TOPOGRAPHY|IMAGERY|GEOLOGY|THERMAL|SPECTRAL|ROVER_OBSERVATION|ATMOSPHERIC|RADIATION|CHEMICAL|SUBSURFACE), mission, instrument, description, scientificPurpose, spatialCoverage (GeoJSON), temporalCoverage, resolution, crs, visualization, attribution, sourceUrl, status. Only expose datasets that actually verified.
• Coordinates: explicit CRS everywhere. Mars geographic (planetocentric vs planetographic, east-positive longitude, 0-360 vs -180/180) must be documented and unit-tested. Define the sphere/ellipsoid radius used for distance and state it. Use GeoJSON for all vector exchange.
• Cache layers: in-memory + disk/SQLite (or Redis if already present) with TTL per data type; serve stale-while-revalidate; every response indicates LIVE vs CACHED and lastSynchronized. Never label cached data as live. Do not store large raw rasters in the app database; store windowed samples/derived products and references.
7. TERRAIN & ROUTE ENGINE (THE SCIENTIFIC CORE)
Terrain derivation (server-side, from the best verified DEM covering the site; document resolution honestly):
• Sample a windowed DEM for the study area; compute slope (e.g., Horn/Zevenbergen-Thorne), aspect, and local roughness proxy where the resolution justifies it.
• Resample to a documented cost-grid resolution; record CRS and cell size.
• Hazard layer: only from verifiable inputs (slope threshold exceedance, no-data masks, geologic-unit flags if justified, imagery-derived exclusions if you implement and validate them). Anything else is not a "hazard."
• LIMITATION always displayed: MOLA-class data does not resolve rocks, small craters, loose sand patches, or boulder fields; HiRISE-scale DTMs may, only where they exist.
Cost model (configurable, documented in docs/ROUTE_ENGINE.md):
cellCost = distanceTerm * (1 + wSlope * f(slope) + wTerrain * g(roughness/unit) + wHazard * hazardFlag) - wScience * scienceOpportunity
• f(slope) should be justified (e.g., a Tobler-style slope-dependent speed/effort function) and clearly marked as an ASSUMPTION adapted from terrestrial walking models, not Mars EVA measurements. Slope limits are configurable assumptions, not NASA-certified values; cite any source you use or label as assumed.
• Cost must remain non-negative for pathfinding (clamp or reformulate science bonus as a multiplicative reduction); explain the choice.
• Algorithm: Dijkstra/A* on an 8- or 16-connected grid, with corridor smoothing; deterministic given inputs.
• Produce at least three candidates with different weight presets: SHORTEST, LOWER MODELED TERRAIN COST, SCIENCE-ORIENTED. Optionally a PAIR/EVA variant with stricter slope limit and a ROVER-ASSISTED variant, both labeled as assumptions.
• Outputs per route: LineString GeoJSON, distance, elevation gain/loss, max and mean modeled slope, cumulative terrain cost, per-segment attributes, waypoints, estimated traverse time only if the speed model is explicitly stated as an assumption, and the list of constraints that were active.
• If no path exists under the constraints, say so and show which constraint blocked it; do not relax silently.
• Include a sensitivity view: how the chosen route changes as weights or the slope limit change.
• Unit tests: known synthetic grid → known optimal path; slope on a plane; coordinate round-trips; no-path case; weight monotonicity.
8. EVIDENCE & TARGET ENGINE
Targets are never arbitrary pins. Pipeline:
OBJECTIVE → required evidence types → spatial overlay of available datasets → candidate scoring → TARGET
• An Objective defines: keywords/geologic-unit relevance rules, required evidence types, optional exclusion rules. These rules are data (JSON/DB), documented, and visible to the user.
• Evidence items reference real records: geologic unit polygon, imagery footprint, Curiosity observation/sol/position, terrain computability. Each has Provenance.
• Candidate scoring is transparent: show each criterion, whether it passed, and the real record behind it. Show only checks the backend can actually support. Score = documented function of evidence; expose weights; no black-box confidence.
• Evidence convergence visual: where several independent layers support an area, render a defined overlap (e.g., evidence-count heat polygon) with a legend; counts derive from real overlaps.
• Every target has WHY HERE, SEE THE EVIDENCE, PLAN A MARSWALK. The Evidence Inspector shows dataset, mission, instrument, contribution, why it matters, source link, retrieval date.
• Honesty rule: these are candidate scientific locations derived from available data and documented rules, not scientific discoveries.
9. ROVER OBSERVATIONS & POV IMAGERY
• Real Curiosity observations by sol, camera/instrument, image URL, and position where verifiable. Position must come from an authoritative traverse/localization source; if an image has no verifiable position, list it as unlocated rather than guessing a location.
• Timeline scrubber by sol; selecting updates map, inspector, and image. Pre-fetch metadata only; load images lazily with proper attribution. Hotlinking: respect source terms; cache thumbnails if permitted.
• POV Preview: for the selected target/route end, show the nearest verified surface imagery with distance-to-target and observation date, labeled clearly as "nearest available rover imagery," never as a rendering of the target. Nothing synthetic.
10. ENVIRONMENT & RADIATION CONTEXT
Only if real data is verified (e.g., MSL RAD dose-rate or REMS series): show as site context, with date range, instrument, units, and the statement that it is historical rover-surface data, not a forecast and not crew dose prediction. If not verified: show a DATA UNAVAILABLE panel that explains what data would be needed. Do not invent a radiation model.
11. WHY SYSTEM, WHAT AM I SEEING, LIMITATIONS
• WHAT AM I SEEING panel appears on every scientific state change with four fixed sections: WHAT YOU ARE SEEING, WHY IT MATTERS, DATA SOURCE, NEXT.
• WHY is first-class: WHY THIS LAYER / REGION / TARGET / ROUTE, always populated from structured state, never free text.
• LIMITATIONS panel is always one click away and also exported in the brief: DATA COVERAGE, RESOLUTION, TEMPORAL LIMITATIONS, MODELING ASSUMPTIONS, MISSING DATA.
12. EXPERIENCE FLOW
Sequence (a mission sequence, not a SaaS progress bar): ORBIT → REGION → DATA → EVIDENCE → TARGET → TERRAIN → ROUTE → MISSION.
Opening: black → Mars appears → "THE FIRST MARSWALK STARTS WITH A MAP." → MARS//PATH → "Plan a scientifically valuable Marswalk using real planetary data." Three entry paths: EXPLORE MARS, FOLLOW THE EVIDENCE, PLAN A MARSWALK. Provide a visible Skip intro and honor prefers-reduced-motion.
3D → 2D continuity: reuse the existing 3D Mars. Rotate to Gale, approach, descend, then crossfade/flatten into the MapLibre map at matching center/zoom/bearing. Both views read and write one shared store (§15). No page switch. If WebGL fails, fall back to the 2D map with a clear notice.
13. MAP & INTERFACE
MapLibre GL JS (or the strongest map stack already in the repo). Real geospatial map: pan, zoom, rotate, coordinates, raster + vector layers, GeoJSON sources, imagery footprints, target/observation markers (clustered or GL symbol layers, never thousands of DOM nodes), routes, measurement tool, feature selection, layer visibility and opacity, search, map-to-data interaction.
• Data rail (left, collapsible): ACTIVE / AVAILABLE by type; each dataset shows WHAT IT SHOWS, WHY IT MATTERS, SOURCE, TECHNICAL DETAILS.
• SIMPLE / ADVANCED toggle, no reload: Advanced exposes mission, instrument, resolution, acquisition time, coverage, projection, processing, product IDs.
• Universal search: locations, regions, features, missions, instruments, datasets, observations, targets, lat/lon. Selection updates state, flies the map, highlights the feature, reveals relevant datasets, explains it, suggests the next step. Feature names must come from a verified gazetteer (e.g., IAU planetary nomenclature via USGS) or be absent.
• Coordinate inspector: LAT, LON, ELEVATION, SLOPE, DATA STATUS. Values only if sampled from real data at that point; otherwise DATA UNAVAILABLE.
• Compare mode: swipe/split with synced cameras and cursors (optical vs. geology, terrain vs. science, route vs. route).
• Elevation profile panel, linked both ways with the map; also shows slope along the route, terrain cost, segment highlighting.
• Decision trail (REGION → OBJECTIVE → EVIDENCE → TARGET → TERRAIN → ROUTE) updated from real state, with a completion marker only when every step is genuinely done.
• Mission brief: editorial "FIELD PLAN" with objective, target, route, distance, evidence, WHY TARGET, WHY ROUTE, data sources with retrieval dates, limitations, NASA disclaimer; export to PDF and JSON (reproducible: includes weights, parameters, dataset versions, and a content hash).
• How this works mode: contextual spotlight on the real interface (not a carousel).
14. PRESENTATION MODES (CHALLENGE ALIGNMENT)
1.  HABITAT PLANNING (default): full desktop planner.
2.  TABLET / FIELD (compact): large touch targets, one active tool, offline-capable cached region pack, high-contrast, route + hazards + POV only.
3.  HUD CONCEPT (stretch): minimal bearing/distance-to-waypoint/slope-ahead overlay driven by the same route data; label it a concept, no fake telemetry.
15. FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE & PERFORMANCE
• Organize by experience/domain (orbit, field, mission; components for map/data/evidence/targets/routes/observations/mission; services for API; geo utilities; hooks). Adapt to the existing repo.
• One shared store (Zustand or existing) for PlanetState { mode, regionId, lat, lon, zoom, bearing, pitch, selectedFeatureId, selectedTargetId, selectedRouteId, activeLayers, layerOpacity, simpleAdvanced, objectiveId }, URL-serializable so any state is shareable and refresh-safe.
• Do not update React state per animation frame; use refs and the render loop for camera/animation.
• Dynamic-import the 3D and map bundles; compress textures; LOD for the globe; Web Workers for heavy client-side geo math; virtualize long lists.
• Budgets: first meaningful paint under 3 s on broadband; map interaction at 60 fps desktop and 30+ fps mid-range mobile; route analysis under 5 s server-side for the demo region (or visible progress); report measured numbers, not estimates.
• Responsive: desktop (map + data rail + inspector + analysis panel), tablet (collapsible rails, bottom sheets), mobile (map-first, one tool at a time).
• Accessibility: keyboard navigation, visible focus, ARIA labels, contrast, prefers-reduced-motion, screen-reader summaries of the selected route/target, and a non-map alternative (tables for targets, routes, observations).
16. BACKEND
Modular API (adapt to the framework): regions, locations, datasets, missions, observations, evidence, targets, terrain/profile, routes/analyze, routes/:id, objectives, mission/brief, search, health. Typed request/response schemas, OpenAPI spec generated or maintained, consistent error envelope {code, message, source, retryable}, request logging, per-source circuit breaker. Required UI states for each feature: LOADING (specific text), EMPTY (with guidance), ERROR (with RETRY), STALE/CACHED (with timestamp).
Database (only entities with real purpose): Region, Dataset, Mission, Instrument, Observation, ScienceTarget, Evidence, Objective, Route, RouteSegment, TerrainAnalysis, MissionPlan, Source. Keep provenance foreign keys. Use migrations. Prefer SQLite/PostgreSQL+PostGIS if already available; otherwise SQLite with GeoJSON columns.
17. OPTIONAL AI LAYER (GROUNDED ONLY)
No generic chatbot. If Gemini features are used, they only transform verified structured state (route metrics, evidence records, dataset metadata) into plain-language explanations, comparisons, and brief text.
• Input contract: JSON of verified results + provenance IDs. Output must cite those IDs; reject or flag any claim not traceable to input.
• Deterministic template fallback when AI is off or fails; the product must be fully functional without any LLM.
• Label AI-written text as AI-GENERATED FROM VERIFIED DATA and show the input it received (expandable).
• AI never chooses routes, scores targets, or produces numbers.
