20. DESIGN LANGUAGE
Planetary atlas + scientific instrument + editorial documentary + mission planning system. Palette: near-black, warm white, Mars red, rust, dark brown, sand, muted gray. Data colors carry scientific meaning (document the legend; use a colorblind-safe scheme for elevation/slope). Type: editorial serif display for narrative, clean sans for UI, monospace for coordinates/metadata. Thin borders, precise grids, hard edges, small radii, negative space; the world stays visually dominant.
Avoid: generic SaaS dashboards, neon/cyberpunk, crypto UI, glassmorphism, big card grids, fake telemetry, decorative glowing lines, gaming-style paths, unexplained animation. Routes are precise, directional, readable lines with waypoints, not effects.
21. EXECUTION PROTOCOL
For every phase: PLAN → IMPLEMENT → TEST → VERIFY → DOCUMENT → REPORT → CONTINUE.
• Phase 0 Audit & plan. Phase 1 Foundation (shell, store, design system, API client, states). Phase 2 Real data (verified adapters, normalization, cache). Phase 3 Map (layers, search, inspector, metadata). Phase 4 Evidence & targets. Phase 5 Terrain & routing. Phase 6 Mission brief & export. Phase 7 Polish (animation, 3D→2D transition, perf, a11y, responsive). Phase 8 Hardening (E2E, honesty tests, performance report, submission pack).
• At the end of each phase, report: what works (demonstrated, with command output or screenshots), what is simulated, what is unavailable, what changed from the plan, and the next phase's risks.
• Cut scope in this order if time runs out: HUD mode → compare mode → sensitivity view → timeline → extra datasets. Never cut: real data, provenance, the evidence→target→route→brief chain, limitations.
• When choosing between approaches, evaluate scientific validity, data availability, performance, reliability, implementation time, maintainability, demo value, UX; record the decision in DECISIONS.md.
• Ask me only for decisions needing human preference or credentials you cannot obtain (e.g., API keys). Otherwise inspect, research, decide, implement, document.
22. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
Success only if all hold:
• Three or more real data kinds from multiple NASA missions are integrated and traceable.
• The map is a real, interactive geospatial map sharing state with the 3D Mars.
• Targets arise from documented rules over real evidence; every check shown is backed by data.
• Routes are computed from real elevation-derived cost with visible methodology, multiple candidates, and an honest explanation of tradeoffs.
• Sources, resolution, and limitations are visible everywhere they matter; no value without provenance.
• A non-expert understands the product within 30 seconds and completes the full journey in 2-3 minutes.
• The app builds, tests pass, E2E passes, and documented performance budgets are met.
• The judge-facing story is clear: This is Mars → real planetary data → combined layers → evidence → target → route candidates → explained tradeoff → mission brief.
